{"seq":1,"slug":"theoretical-mechanics","title":"理论力学","docCount":3,"url":"/l-doc-books/education/university/natural-sciences/mechanics/theoretical-mechanics","docs":[{"seq":1,"slug":"statics-equilibrium","title":"静力学与受力平衡","url":"/l-doc-books/education/university/natural-sciences/mechanics/theoretical-mechanics/statics-equilibrium","wordCount":1270,"readingTime":6},{"seq":2,"slug":"kinematics-of-motion","title":"运动学：只描述运动，不问原因","url":"/l-doc-books/education/university/natural-sciences/mechanics/theoretical-mechanics/kinematics-of-motion","wordCount":1266,"readingTime":6},{"seq":3,"slug":"dynamics-and-energy","title":"动力学：力、能量与守恒","url":"/l-doc-books/education/university/natural-sciences/mechanics/theoretical-mechanics/dynamics-and-energy","wordCount":1266,"readingTime":6}],"introHtml":"<h1 id=\"理论力学\">理论力学</h1><p>理论力学是我学力学时遇到的第一道坎，也是回报最高的一块。它的核心假设很大胆：把所有物体都当成&quot;刚体&quot;——一个受力也绝不变形的理想模型。这听起来不真实，但正是这个简化，让我们能把注意力集中在力和运动的关系上，不被材料的伸缩干扰。等地基打牢了，后面材料力学再把&quot;会变形&quot;这个现实加回来。</p>\n<p>我把理论力学分成三篇来学，对应它内部最经典的三分法。</p>\n<p>第一篇是<strong>静力学</strong>。它问的是：一个物体要想保持静止或匀速，受到的力必须满足什么条件？答案是合力为零、合力矩也为零。听起来简单，但真正的功夫在于&quot;受力分析&quot;——把一个物体从环境里&quot;抠&quot;出来，画出它受到的每一个力。我吃过的亏几乎都来自漏画或多画了某个力，所以这篇会反复强调隔离体图的画法。</p>\n<p>第二篇是<strong>运动学</strong>。它只描述运动，不追问原因。位置、速度、加速度之间的微分与积分关系，直线运动、曲线运动、转动，都在这里。运动学像是给运动拍了一段精确的影片，告诉你物体在每一刻在哪、走多快，但不解释是什么力造成的。</p>\n<p>第三篇是<strong>动力学</strong>。这是把前两篇接上的关键一步：牛顿第二定律 <span class=\"math-inline\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo>⃗</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>a</mi><mo>⃗</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{F}=m\\vec{a}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9663em;\"></span><span class=\"mord accent\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.9663em;\"><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span><span style=\"top:-3.2523em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"accent-body\" style=\"left:-0.1522em;\"><span class=\"overlay\" style=\"height:0.714em;width:0.471em;\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"0.471em\" height=\"0.714em\" style=\"width:0.471em\" viewBox=\"0 0 471 714\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMinYMin\"><path d=\"M377 20c0-5.333 1.833-10 5.5-14S391 0 397 0c4.667 0 8.667 1.667 12 5\n3.333 2.667 6.667 9 10 19 6.667 24.667 20.333 43.667 41 57 7.333 4.667 11\n10.667 11 18 0 6-1 10-3 12s-6.667 5-14 9c-28.667 14.667-53.667 35.667-75 63\n-1.333 1.333-3.167 3.5-5.5 6.5s-4 4.833-5 5.5c-1 .667-2.5 1.333-4.5 2s-4.333 1\n-7 1c-4.667 0-9.167-1.833-13.5-5.5S337 184 337 178c0-12.667 15.667-32.333 47-59\nH213l-171-1c-8.667-6-13-12.333-13-19 0-4.667 4.333-11.333 13-20h359\nc-16-25.333-24-45-24-59z\"/></svg></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.714em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span><span class=\"mord accent\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.714em;\"><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span></span><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"accent-body\" style=\"left:-0.2355em;\"><span class=\"overlay\" style=\"height:0.714em;width:0.471em;\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"0.471em\" height=\"0.714em\" style=\"width:0.471em\" viewBox=\"0 0 471 714\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMinYMin\"><path d=\"M377 20c0-5.333 1.833-10 5.5-14S391 0 397 0c4.667 0 8.667 1.667 12 5\n3.333 2.667 6.667 9 10 19 6.667 24.667 20.333 43.667 41 57 7.333 4.667 11\n10.667 11 18 0 6-1 10-3 12s-6.667 5-14 9c-28.667 14.667-53.667 35.667-75 63\n-1.333 1.333-3.167 3.5-5.5 6.5s-4 4.833-5 5.5c-1 .667-2.5 1.333-4.5 2s-4.333 1\n-7 1c-4.667 0-9.167-1.833-13.5-5.5S337 184 337 178c0-12.667 15.667-32.333 47-59\nH213l-171-1c-8.667-6-13-12.333-13-19 0-4.667 4.333-11.333 13-20h359\nc-16-25.333-24-45-24-59z\"/></svg></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> 把&quot;受了什么力&quot;和&quot;会怎么动&quot;连了起来。但牛顿定律只是入口，动力学真正威力体现在功、能、动量这些守恒量上——很多用牛顿定律要解半天的题，用能量守恒一行就出来了。</p>\n<p>学这一章时我的体会是：不要急着套公式，先把&quot;研究对象是谁、它受了哪些力、用哪条定律最省事&quot;这三件事想清楚。一旦养成这个习惯，后面所有力学分支都会变得顺手。</p>\n"}